  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Cherry Gall Azure is a small butterfly belonging to the Gossamer-winged family (Lycaenidae), characterized by delicate wings and a preference for cherry trees in New England. This species is named for its association with cherry galls, which serve as important host plants for its larvae.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Wing Color and Pattern",
      "description": "Males display bright blue upperwings with white fringes, while females are predominantly gray-brown with blue scaling near the body",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Small Size",
      "description": "This butterfly is quite tiny, with a wingspan of approximately 0.75 to 1 inch, making it one of the smaller blues in the region",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Underside Markings",
      "description": "The ventral (underside) surface displays pale gray wings with faint dark spots and a white or silvery appearance",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Cherry Gall Azure butterflies are found in deciduous and mixed forests throughout New England, particularly in areas where wild cherry trees and cherry galls are abundant.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The larvae of Cherry Gall Azure develop inside cherry tree galls, using these abnormal plant growths as both shelter and food source—a specialized ecological relationship that makes this species dependent on the health of cherry tree populations.",
